    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d42">

                <pb facs="0134" n="52" sameAs="frus:frus1925v02/pg52"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">839.51/2673</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Dominican Minister</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Ariza</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Translation<anchor n="8" xml:id="d42-f1"
                    />]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1925-10-05"><hi rendition="#italic">October 5,
                        1925</hi></date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Mr. Secretary of State</hi>: I have the honor to
                    forward herewith to you a certified copy of Resolution No. 179 passed by the
                    Dominican <pb facs="0135" n="53" xml:id="pg53"/> Congress on the 23rd and 25th
                    of May, 1925,<anchor n="9" xml:id="d42-f2"/> carrying approval of the Convention
                    concluded between the United States of America and the Dominican Republic on the
                    27th of December, 1924.</p>
                <p>This Resolution also contains several explanations that the Dominican Congress
                    saw fit to give with a proposal similar to that expressed by the said Congress
                    in its approval of the Dominican-American Convention of February 8, 1907, that
                    is to say, with the understanding that these explanations in no wise change the
                    text of the Convention and only serve to dispel any doubt that may arise
                    concerning its interpretation.</p>
                <p>By virtue of that Resolution and in accordance with instructions from my
                    Government, I have the honor to submit the said Resolution, through Your
                    Excellency’s worthy medium, to the approval of the Government of the United
                    States.</p>
                <p>I avail myself [etc.]</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">J. C. Ariza</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
